Z||000017||88formula||01-14-2001||06:10 PM||||I did the math on those  Silvolite 1486 pistons with the 12-cc head volume and I got 8.39-1 compression. That is if the fiero heads are 57 cc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Shaun41178||01-15-2001||01:31 AM||||wow travis only has a 2.8?  I thought you had put a 3.1 in that engine basterd.  hmm ok well 15x hp at the wheels is pretty impressive.  I made 154 at the hweels with a 50 shot of nitrous on my 2.8.  It makes me wonder just how much that e-ram adds.<P>The cc's of the FIero heads are 52 cc not 57cc||||reg||1
Z||000019||Formula 18||01-15-2001||02:41 AM||||I just did the math <BR>___________54cc head____ 57cc head___ 55ccHead <BR>_____________KB 1486____ KB 1486____ KB 1496 <BR>Bore:_________ 3.504 ____ 3.504____ 3.504<BR>Stroke:_______ 3.31______ 3.31 ____ 3.31<BR>Deak Height *: .030 ____ .030 ____ .030<BR>Gasket Thinkness:.030 _ .030 ____ .030<BR>Head Volume**: ___  64 cc    ____    69cc  ____     81cc<P><BR>CR        ____         8.11    ____    7.66   ____    6.77<P><BR>* Deck Height--&gt; Distance top of piston stops from top of bore. I guessed .030 of an iin<P>** Head Volume--&gt; Cylinder head combustion chamber volume in Cubic Centimeter's<P><BR>All value calculated by Rall Hall Turbofast site. <A HREF="http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFcomp.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFcomp.html</A> <P>The last one is what happenes when you put iron heads on a AL headed block. <P>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 01-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Shaun41178||01-15-2001||03:30 AM||||There is a forum member on here that I think his name is Jeff.  anyways he has the KB pistons and the company says they give an 8.8:1 compression ratio with the stock setup<P>Like i said before piston manufactureres do not make pistons with a lower compression ratio then what comes from the factory.  If they did they wouldn't be able to sell any except to turbo owners.  The fiero heads are 52cc stock.  shave them and you might get them to 50 or less maybe.  <P>The TRW pistons that most use with the 3.1 conversion have no lower then a 8.5:1 compression ratio from the manufacturer.||||reg||1
Z||000021||1FST2M6||01-15-2001||10:37 AM||||up from 103hp and 132lbft ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spiraldyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/spiraldyno.jpg</A>  )with the changing of the cat from 1 7/8"(not stock, WAY to small and was clogged so bad it made the manifolds GLOW!!) to 2.5", Identical chip that GT Bastard is using in his car,(similar to an ADS Strip has the same off idle #s but go's FULL rich send the ign to TDC at WOT above/at 3200rpm) an APEXi AFC tuning the fuel. so i don't totally kill the new cat. I'm nearly at the HP numbers my Nitrous car had... just don't have the torque... but thats ok.. we'll work on that... only bad thing is the car knocks and pings really bad with out Octane additive and premium fuel... so my $12 fill ups just went to nearly $20<P>but the heads are shaved, I'm running an Accel ICM, Wide Band O2 from Competition Data Syatems, Crane PS91 coil, (for sale MSD Blaster Coil stock replacement $20)  <P><BR>you can see on the dyno sheet we still have to work the bugs out of the igntion cause it cuts out and get choppy above 3200. running it at TDC the motors not happy with the igntion arangement but it pulls like a freight train! <P>dyno sheet to follow in an hour .. the scanners here at work are tied up... <BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||lou_dias||01-15-2001||12:17 PM||||my 150 rwhp 3.4+ gt runs fine in 87 octane||||reg||1
Z||000023||1FST2M6||01-15-2001||02:30 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstdyno_manymods.jpg">  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Steven||01-15-2001||03:00 PM||||Don't know if anyone else cares, but i bought the seal power piston to a 3.1 Firebird(made by federal mogul), and thought that I was sent the wrong pistons because they had a dish to them, and the stock 2.8 are flat topped. Came to work today, double check to see what the 91-92 f-body used for .030 o/s or 1mm o/s. GM show those pistons to by part number 10100066. Pulled the prints on that part, and they show a dished piston. Volume of dish is 10.64 cc.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000025||88formula||01-15-2001||05:41 PM||||If the head volume is 52 cc and the piston is .030 down the bore at TDC than I get a compression ratio of 8.95-1. The volume of the dish on the Silvolite 1486 piston is 12 cc according to the silvolite web-site. <A HREF="http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kb-silvolite.com/slvpg23.htm</A> <P><BR>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Steven||01-15-2001||06:50 PM||||Also the GM service manual for the '91 Firebird show the 3.1L engine as having 8.5:1 compression ratio. If someone has that book, it is on page 6a2-31. Third line form the bottom. ||||reg||4
Z||000027||GT Bastard||01-15-2001||07:01 PM||||Trav, that's impressive!  You really made that car into a movin' machine...  Got any tipes for me?  hehe.  Does it feel faster than my SE did while off nitrous?||||reg||1
Z||000028||lou_dias||01-15-2001||10:24 PM||||Trav, what set up is that.  I think you still have my dyno run with that damn spiralmax in where I made 146.? hp and 203 lbs of torque.  Make me a turbo kit, PLEASE!||||reg||1
Z||000029||1FST2M6||01-16-2001||08:27 AM||||it doesn't "feel" faster. i think cause it's an automatic.. it just goes.. but putz'n along and stomping on it to pass somone like, avoiding sitting at another light and you see the other lane is free; then it feels fast.. the tires are searching for grip, the exhaust is waking up the neighbors and the gimp in the 89 240SX is crying... cause i just WAXED him with his girlfriend in his car!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Not sure what you've done to the motor Jeff but  <P>YA DID DAMN GOOD!<P>No real tips just keep the 2.5" ex, that chip is worth it's weight in gold, and let me know when your done with the motor install and I'll set up a dyno tuning session somwhere close to ya. I really think the key to the 2.8 power is running it as close to TDC as possible. the SE was/is a MONSTER at that and the GT is turning into one... we are gunna mess around with Predators car when it's back together and see how the turbo runs at stock ign, then TDC. My 87 Supra ran at TDC under full boost...so why not a Turbo Fiero! Now if i could just make the gas cheaper so i could afford to drive it more... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||GT Bastard||01-16-2001||07:22 PM||||When I saw your new dyno numbers, I about sh!t myself.  Like I said, it was just milled .020 over, valves were redone, new springs, and new seals.  My dad said he removed 2 cupfuls of iron from those heads but kept that little hump in the ports.  The only other thing done to the motor is the ported exhaust manifolds.  I know that many will say the ass-o-meter is not accurate, but the car pulled noticeably harder after I got it back together.  Even Fierogod(Brad) said it felt freakishly fast for an automatic, and he's driven countless other Fiero's.  I could get it sideways pretty easily coming around a corner, and sometimes it chirped the tires into second.||||reg||1
Z||000031||88formula||01-16-2001||07:22 PM||||Hey Travis, what do you mean by running the 2.8 at TDC? Do you mean you are running 0 ignition advance? You got me scratching my head on that one!||||reg||1
Z||000032||1FST2M6||01-17-2001||08:40 AM||||yeah the APEXi AFC and the new chip (nearly the same as GT Bastards). make the motor run a 0* advance timing above 3200rpm(or as close as it can get to 0*) the rest of the time it runs at 14*adv. giving the motor the highest possible compression at the time the sparkplugs fire. it's hard on the engine, crappy on gas and has issues with cheap gas and throws codes all day long. But it's faster! I gained 54hp and 36lbft by forcing the car to run there... well in addition to the larger cat and fuel tuning.  <P>if you look at the dyno sheet you can see the engine and ignition really aren't happy about it... they both cut up pretty good.. (graphs not as smooth as the lower RPM range)and with no revlimiter the automaic really isn't as bad as i thought it was gunna be...||||reg||1
Z||000033||88formula||01-17-2001||06:23 PM|||| WOW! Of all the research and reading I have done and all the real dyno’s I have witnessed and all the schooling I had in automotive I have never seen a motor make more power with 0 ignition advance above 3200 rpm than it did with ignition advance. That is with the exception of a nitrous motor because of detonation. Usually you get more cylinder pressure with more timing advance as the engine climbs in rpm. You want the fuel to be mostly burnt by the time the piston reaches TDC so you get the most pressure from the fuel like you said right? I have always thought and have been taught that as the cylinder goes up and down faster you need more ignition advance because the fuel burns at a constant rate. If the fuel mixture is ignited @ TDC when the motor is above 3200 rpm than by the time the fuel is completely burned the piston is already halfway back down the cylinder and the exhaust valve starts to open. You could easily burn your exhaust valves running your motor like that because the fuel is still burning as it goes through the exhaust port. I’m going to have to tell my machinist who builds $30,000 race engines with 20+ years experience and has an engine dynamometer about this one.<P>Do your manifolds glow red? I see the dyno sheet you posted but I just cannot believe that it is because you are running 0 ignition advance above 3200 rpm. If it were a nitrous motor the torque would be much higher right? Maybe I have misunderstood something Travis. I know you have been tuning engines for a long time but I am having a little trouble understanding how an internal combustion engine makes more power with 0 ignition advance above 3200 rpm. How is this possible? Help me out on this one!<P><BR>Thanks<P><BR>Yeahhhhhhhhhhh 1000 I hit the 1000 post mark.   <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||GT Bastard||01-18-2001||08:52 AM||||Well, I'm no engine expert and won't pretend to be.  All I know is that my SE(Travs old car) will HUMILIATE a stock 2.8, and doesn't have a problem pulling on a cammed 3.1 Fiero with a 4sp.  And it murders just about anything off the line(afterwords is another story.)  And this is with a 2.8 that's been through hell and back(30+ bottles of N20, countless dyno runs, and lots of racing.) The motor itself is stock except for the valves and springs(correct me if I'm wrong Trav.)  Also keep in mind that the shop Travis works in(Engineered Performance) builds race cars, and they're all over Grassroots Motorsports magazine, so they must be doing something right.  I've met Trav in person and corresponded with him via email on numerous occasions, so I know that he's the no nonsense type.  When something works, he shows dyno-runs to prove it.  That's my rant...<P>||||reg||1
